{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And He said, For I will be with you.</b> He answered him point by point — for the first, a first answer, and for the last, a last. You said, 'Who am I that I should go to Pharaoh?' — it is not your own mission but Mine: 'For I will be with you.' <b>And this</b> — the vision you saw in the bush — <b>shall be the sign for you that I have sent you:</b> I am worthy to deliver. Just as you saw the bush performing My mission and not being consumed, so you will go on My mission and come to no harm. And as for what you asked — what merit do Israel have that they should go out of Egypt? I have a great purpose for this exodus, for they are destined to receive the Torah on this mountain at the end of three months after they leave Egypt. Another interpretation: <b>For I will be with you, and this</b> — that you will succeed in your mission — <b>shall be the sign for you</b> of another promise, which I promise you: when you bring them out of Egypt, you will serve Me on this mountain — that you will receive the Torah upon it; and that is the merit that stands for Israel. A parallel to this expression we find: ״וְזֶה לְךָ הָאוֹת אָכוֹל הַשָּׁנָה סָפִיחַ וגו׳״ (Isaiah 37) — the fall of Sennacherib shall be a sign for you of another promise, that your land is bereft of fruit yet I will bless the after-growth."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:50:24.707844869Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}